WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.267 --> 00:00:01.800
Can I just, erm...

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:03.467
Don't... I don't know what to...

00:00:03.500 --> 00:00:05.167
Erm...

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:06.400
Erm...

00:00:06.433 --> 00:00:07.600
I think, er...

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:11.000
I don't know what to say.
I'm sorry.

00:00:18.233 --> 00:00:20.267
You don't know sometimes
what to say

00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:22.533
and you're basically
walking on eggshells.

00:00:22.567 --> 00:00:23.767
Sometimes it can shock people.

00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:25.367
Someone says,
"Are you having a good day?"

00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:28.067
They just expect you to say,
"Yes, I'm OK."

00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:30.233
And I've said, "No, I'm not."

00:00:30.267 --> 00:00:32.567
And some people can get taken aback,
"Oh, oh, oh."

00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:35.300
And I think there's
the fear of saying the wrong thing.

00:00:35.333 --> 00:00:37.633
You know, "What do I know
about mental health?"

00:00:43.367 --> 00:00:45.633
I was diagnosed
with anorexia nervosa,

00:00:45.667 --> 00:00:47.300
body dysmorphic disorder

00:00:47.333 --> 00:00:51.067
and obsessive compulsive
disorder back in 2013.

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:56.133
And on Friday, the 29th of August,
2014, I tried to take my own life.

00:00:56.167 --> 00:00:59.033
My name's Harkiran.
I'm Sandeep's mum.

00:00:59.067 --> 00:01:00.433
Mother, is it?

00:01:00.467 --> 00:01:01.533
Best friend.

00:01:01.567 --> 00:01:03.333
And best friend.

00:01:03.367 --> 00:01:08.500
Dave first joined a MINT session
when he was struggling

00:01:08.533 --> 00:01:10.633
with his mental health.

00:01:10.667 --> 00:01:12.467
And from there, we kind of -

00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:15.100
in a strange way - hit it off,
I think.

00:01:15.133 --> 00:01:16.267
It was love at first sight.

00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:18.367
I like to think of it
that way!

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:19.800
I'm Dr Will Murcott

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:22.333
and I'm a mental health nurse
and senior lecturer

00:01:22.367 --> 00:01:23.700
with The Open University.

00:01:33.033 --> 00:01:35.133
For me, listening is
at the top of the list.

00:01:35.167 --> 00:01:38.300
I think that's the first
and most important step.

00:01:38.333 --> 00:01:41.600
If you can, active listening -
repeating back some of the things

00:01:41.633 --> 00:01:44.200
that they've said to you so
that they can feel and realise

00:01:44.233 --> 00:01:45.433
that they're being helped.

00:01:45.467 --> 00:01:48.633
That first initial
conversation that you had with me,

00:01:48.667 --> 00:01:51.000
when my mum and dad sat me down,

00:01:51.033 --> 00:01:53.533
that's when the bubble popped
for me.

00:01:53.567 --> 00:01:57.300
That's when I thought,
at that very moment, that

00:01:57.333 --> 00:01:59.600
someone's noticing how I'm feeling,

00:01:59.633 --> 00:02:04.200
someone is noticing and having
an oversight of my pain,

00:02:04.233 --> 00:02:06.433
just asking how you are.

00:02:06.467 --> 00:02:09.400
It was so crucial and so important
to me at that moment.

00:02:09.433 --> 00:02:10.800
Ask twice, you know,

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:13.000
"Are you OK?"
"Yeah, I'm all right."

00:02:13.033 --> 00:02:14.700
"But, no, are you really OK?"

00:02:14.733 --> 00:02:17.400
And then it's like,
"Well, no, actually, I'm not."

00:02:17.433 --> 00:02:18.700
I think there is an urge

00:02:18.733 --> 00:02:22.067
to try and problem-solve
and do something,

00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:24.200
and there's almost a need sometimes

00:02:24.233 --> 00:02:28.767
to be able to fix a problem
or a person,

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:33.367
and sometimes that's not the right
thing to do in that situation.

00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:36.000
People aren't necessarily
looking for answers.

00:02:36.033 --> 00:02:37.600
People want to be heard.

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:44.333
One of the things
I've learned as a mental health

00:02:44.367 --> 00:02:49.467
professional is that often if I try
and set goals or have my own needs

00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:54.200
and wants and desires
for how somebody should get better,

00:02:54.233 --> 00:02:56.533
they are usually wrong.

00:02:57.633 --> 00:03:01.533
And so, from my point of view,
it's working with a person

00:03:01.567 --> 00:03:06.233
at their pace and trying to
understand how they want to move.

00:03:06.267 --> 00:03:10.767
You can suggest go to your GP,
you can suggest support groups,

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:13.233
you can suggest people
take in an activity.

00:03:13.267 --> 00:03:15.367
You can go and join in
that activity with them

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:17.633
so they're not feeling alone
with that.

00:03:17.667 --> 00:03:19.633
One size does not fit all.

00:03:19.667 --> 00:03:23.167
You had a really great idea, didn't
you? The family trusted circle.

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:27.333
Yes, so what we do, we go
out the family environment and go to

00:03:27.367 --> 00:03:31.567
a coffee shop or just sit there,
all four of us,

00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:33.767
actually doing our pos and negs.

00:03:33.800 --> 00:03:36.733
"What's been your positive
for the whole month?

00:03:36.767 --> 00:03:38.400
"What's been your negative
for the month?"

00:03:38.433 --> 00:03:40.800
We spend a lot of time
insulting each other.

00:03:43.067 --> 00:03:45.400
We just send each other
little videos or...

00:03:45.433 --> 00:03:47.767
"Yeah, I've been watching that,"
and just little clips.

00:03:47.800 --> 00:03:49.733
Everybody's at the click
of a button now.

00:03:49.767 --> 00:03:51.633
- You're in my pocket.
- Yeah.

00:03:55.233 --> 00:03:58.433
If they're really
struggling and at breaking point,

00:03:58.467 --> 00:04:01.633
emergency phone lines out there.

00:04:01.667 --> 00:04:04.067
- Samaritans.
- And Samaritans as well.

00:04:04.100 --> 00:04:05.800
They need to know that...

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:07.500
There is someone out there.

00:04:07.533 --> 00:04:09.733
..there is support out there.

00:04:09.767 --> 00:04:15.033
And it's so hard,
so hard to see that when...

00:04:15.067 --> 00:04:16.400
You are in a dark place.

00:04:16.433 --> 00:04:18.067
..when you're in a dark place, yeah.

00:04:18.100 --> 00:04:21.700
One of the scary things
is that kind of pressure of,

00:04:21.733 --> 00:04:23.267
"What do I do if...?"

00:04:23.300 --> 00:04:26.600
because there can be nothing more
scary than having somebody talk

00:04:26.633 --> 00:04:32.133
to you about ending their life,
harming themselves in some way.

00:04:32.167 --> 00:04:37.100
Really, the person needs to know
as well at what point you'll find

00:04:37.133 --> 00:04:41.433
something acceptable or unacceptable
and then have to do something

00:04:41.467 --> 00:04:42.533
or involve somebody else.

00:04:42.567 --> 00:04:44.800
I always try and get that
conversation done as soon

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:49.700
as I can to say, "Look, you know,
I will never breach your confidence,

00:04:49.733 --> 00:04:53.667
"but if there are things
that worry me, then...

00:04:55.067 --> 00:04:58.267
"..then I may have to do
something about that."

00:04:58.300 --> 00:05:01.067
And again, that's absolutely OK

00:05:01.100 --> 00:05:03.333
because the person will thank you
for it afterwards.

00:05:03.367 --> 00:05:05.033
Let people know that,

00:05:05.067 --> 00:05:09.067
"I'm here and we're going to do
this together," so if that means,

00:05:09.100 --> 00:05:11.667
"Right, me and you, we're going
to make an appointment at the GP,

00:05:11.700 --> 00:05:14.467
we'll go together
and we'll do this together."

00:05:14.500 --> 00:05:17.033
And it's about being
in the mud together.

00:05:20.167 --> 00:05:23.067
You have to keep your own
emotional batteries charged up,

00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:25.800
cos when they're drained out,
you can't help anybody else

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:27.600
and you struggle to function,

00:05:27.633 --> 00:05:30.467
let alone just trying
to look after yourself.

00:05:30.500 --> 00:05:34.467
The plane and the oxygen mask -
put your oxygen mask on first.

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:38.233
Self-care is
the number-one priority, I think.

00:05:42.567 --> 00:05:44.233
So, I don't think
you can actually fail,

00:05:44.267 --> 00:05:46.800
and that's something
that I would really push to

00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:50.033
everyone who's helping someone
with mental health struggles,

00:05:50.067 --> 00:05:56.767
is you haven't failed, you cannot
fail, but you can do something.

00:05:56.800 --> 00:05:59.600
Trust... Again, trust
your instincts. Be yourself.

00:05:59.633 --> 00:06:02.600
The right thing
is probably what you first felt,

00:06:02.633 --> 00:06:06.400
and that could have been sitting
and listening, just nodding,

00:06:06.433 --> 00:06:10.500
or it could have been responding,
"Tell me more about that."

00:06:10.533 --> 00:06:15.167
So, not to over-worry, not to
overthink, but there is support

00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:18.067
out there and there are people
that will listen to you

00:06:18.100 --> 00:06:19.800
and give you advice and guidance.

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:21.333
And it's important to talk.

00:06:21.367 --> 00:06:23.500
Oh, my God,
it's so important to talk.

00:06:23.533 --> 00:06:24.733
Very important to talk.

00:06:24.767 --> 00:06:27.633
We end most chats with,
"Love you, man."

00:06:27.667 --> 00:06:29.567
You know, it's...

00:06:29.600 --> 00:06:30.600
Yeah, it's good to...

00:06:30.633 --> 00:06:32.333
It's good to have someone
like Josh in my life.

00:06:32.367 --> 00:06:34.800
- It makes my life better.
- And vice versa.

